("p", "The formal situation is invariant across the corpus. There is a hidden world or history h, and an observational record O(h) — what the sensors return, what the prefix of the past permits, what the finite state can retain, what the interface transmits. A deterministic implementable policy must factor through that record: π = π′ ∘ O. If two histories produce the same available information, no policy can respond differently to them. In the sequential setting, “available information” means the relevant observational history, not merely the current sensor reading; memory can preserve an earlier distinction, but it cannot manufacture a distinction that was never sensed."),
("p", "Impose a finite-state implementation and a second loss of distinctions appears. The controller no longer retains the entire observational history; it maintains a recursively updateable summary, q<sub>t+1</sub> = δ(q<sub>t</sub>, o<sub>t</sub>). Observation identifies hidden histories that the sensors cannot distinguish, and finite memory identifies observed histories that the implementation cannot retain separately. These are related but different limitations, and they have different repairs: more memory can repair forgetting, never blindness. Moreover, not every compression of histories is implementable sequentially — the summary must be updateable from its current state and the next input alone. Causality constrains the representation itself, not only the behaviour. This is the deepest bridge between the two pillars: both ask whether behaviour remains realizable after distinctions have been identified, but they constrain different stages of the identification."),
("p", "Two currencies govern everything in the corpus. Width, or rate, is the capacity of the interface: log|O|, treewidth, state count, Hankel rank, determination index, Weihrauch degree — each domain names the same quantity in its own units. Distortion, or obstruction, is the deficiency of the lift: a fibre conflict, an obstruction datum, a singular value σ<sub>k</sub>, an inner/outer gap. Every theorem in the corpus is a statement about one currency conditional on the other: either a width that suffices for a given distortion, or a distortion floor that no given width can beat. Figure 1 shows the architecture these two currencies organize."),
