("p", "Two rows are the non-obvious ones, and they carry the most weight. Bell locality: a local hidden-variable model is exactly a global policy in which each party’s output, given the hidden variable, is uniform across the other party’s setting — nonlocality is a factorization failure, which is why the CHSH inequality functions as a certificate in the same slot a Farkas vector occupies elsewhere. Computability: computable maps on infinite data are continuous — each finite piece of output is uniform across inputs that agree to some finite precision — so the “uniform” of observational uniformity and the “uniform” of Weihrauch’s uniform reducibility are the same word for the same reason. The title of the first pillar does double duty in the same way: “effective” names both effective descent in the categorical sense and effectivity in the computable sense."),
