("p", "Reading the corpora together exposes a structural fact invisible from inside any one of them: the feasible use of a bottleneck is cheap; choosing the bottleneck is hard; and even the best choice has an analytic floor. Hence two independent lower-bound technologies, which the programme shows to be the two walls of the same room. The combinatorial wall: sensor design and sensor repair are W[2]-hard, admit no better than logarithmic approximation unless P = NP, and are ETH-tight — the signature of hitting set. The analytic wall: AAK/Hankel floors — even the right quotient of a given width cannot beat σ<sub>k</sub>. Neither wall is visible from inside a single work; together they say that partial observability is not a nuisance to be engineered away but a conserved quantity with a price schedule."),
("p", "The reason for the division of labour is precise, and it belongs to fable’s synthesis: a partition quotient is a 0/1 projection, and a subspace quotient is an orthogonal projection; relaxing the former to the latter converts hitting-set into an eigenvalue problem. The integrality gap between partition and subspace quotients is therefore the price of physical realizability. The practical consequence is a division of labour that runs through the whole corpus: choosing the sensor is provably hard even to approximate, while pricing it is spectral and computable in time polynomial in the size of a finite section — the converses do not solve design, but they bound it from below cheaply."),
